{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for systematic underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slowly rising) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (spikes to 76+ and drops to 68+). Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range to capture these swings.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows consecutive high values (1.00) followed by a price spike, the model fails to anticipate the subsequent sharp correction. Reduce forecasts immediately after a spike occurs, rather than maintaining the upward drift.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to downward trends, often continuing to forecast increases or stability while prices drop. If the last 3 days of history show a decline, force the forecast to decline by at least 1.5x the historical daily average drop.",
    "Incorporate sentiment decay: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history often precede short-term price peaks followed by immediate drops. Do not extrapolate the momentum from high sentiment; instead, treat high sentiment as a contrarian signal for a near-term pullback.",
    "Horizon-specific bias correction: The model's 30-day forecast is biased towards the mean of the recent history (approx 70-71) and ignores the structural shift to higher volatility. Shift the baseline forecast up by 2-3 EUR to account for the new higher volatility regime, but apply a dampening factor to the slope to prevent overshooting the peaks."
  ]
}
